{
  "text": "2007A16 Briefly\noutline the components of parenteral nutrition, explaining the rationale\nfor the use of each component.\nEnergy\n·\n8.4MJ/day\n·\nBody can adapt to various macronutrient contributions\nWater\n·\nDaily requirement 2-2.5L/day\n·\n1.5L urine (minimum 0.6L)\n·\n0.4L resp tract\n·\n0.4L skin transepidermal diffusion\n·\n0.2L faeces\nCarbohydrate\n·\n17kJ/g\n·\nSupplies 50% energy\n·\nBrain and RBC obligate (brain also ketones)\nProtein\n·\n16kJ/g\n·\nSupplies 20% energy\n·\n20 essential amino acids required\n·\nMinimum ~0.5g/kg/day\n·\nGlutamine catabolized readily in fasting state\nFat\n·\n37kJ/g\n·\n30% energy requirement\n·\ne.g. linoleic acid\nVitamins\n-Fat soluble\n-Water soluble\n·\nA (e.g. eyes), D (bone density, Ca\n2+\nhomeostasis), E\n  (e.g skin), K (coagulation)\n·\nB: many subtypes, e.g. B12 cofactor for methionine synthetase (DNA\n  synthesis)\n·\nC: wound healing, skin health\nElectrolytes\n·\nNa\n+\n: nerve action potentials, ECF osmolality\n·\nK\n+\n: nerve action potential, ICF osmolality\n·\nCa\n2+\n: muscle contraction\n·\nMg\n2+\n: Enzyme cofactor e.g. Na\n+\nK\n+\nATPase\n·\nCl\n-\n: ECF osmolyte\n·\nPO\n4\n3-\n: ICF for ATP synthesis and buffering\nMinerals\n·\nFe\n2+\n: for Hb synthesis, cytochrome synthesis\n·\nZn\n2+\n: cofactor in enzymatic reactions\n·\nI\n-\n: thyroid hormone synthesis\nSpecial populations\n·\nPregnancy: 1.2 x BMR, ↑↑protein requirement\n·\nNeonate: 2x BMR, ↑requirement of all\n·\nElderly: BMR decreased\n·\nSepsis: BMR increased, catabolic state, ↑protein requirement\n·\nBurns: catabolic state, ↑protein requirement\n·\nLarge bone fracture: hypermetabolic and anabolic state, ↑BMR and\n  protein requirement\nFeedback welcome at\nketaminenightmares@gmail.com",
